course: dictionary
lesson: 337
title: Annexation: when land changes hands
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Annexation is a long word, but its clues are in the sentences around it. Think about what happens to the land. In this lesson, the land is Crimea. «Russian troops without insignia seized Crimea» tells you that troops took control of the place. Then «annexation (Russia declaring the land its own)» tells you what that taking really means. The brackets are a clue: annexation means a country declares land its own.
explain: Annexation has a second meaning too. A town or city can join nearby land to itself by law. This is about rules and papers, not soldiers. So annexation can be done by force, or by law. The word itself is the same, so the context must tell you which kind is meant.
explain: Some clues in the text warn you that something is wrong. «The UN General Assembly called the referendum invalid» means the vote was judged not to count. Most countries at the United Nations said the annexation was illegal. When you see words like illegal or invalid, you know the author is judging the action, not just describing it.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Annexation was condemned as illegal» still makes sense if you read it as «taking land by force and making it your own was condemned as illegal.» If your meaning fits, you have probably got it right.
fact: Annexation is a noun. It is the act of annexing, which means taking control of something and making it part of yourself.
fact: «Annexation» means one country takes control of land that belonged to another country and makes it its own, often by force.
fact: «Annexation» can also mean a town or city joins nearby land to itself by law.
fact: In the example, «troops without insignia» means soldiers without badges, so nobody could see clearly whose soldiers they were.
fact: History has many annexations by empires. Looking for the word annexation in a history book is a good way to find where land changed hands.
exercise: «A referendum was held under occupation, and then annexation followed.» What does annexation mean here? | *Taking control of land and making it part of your own country | Soldiers going home after a war | Holding a vote to choose a new football team | A friendly deal between two people who shake hands
exercise: «The UN General Assembly called the referendum invalid.» What does invalid mean? | Extremely loud | Very valid and official | *Not valid, so it does not count | Full of invalids who need help
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Russian troops without insignia seized Crimea» that means took control of quickly and by force. | insignia | Crimea | troops | *seized
test: A town joins nearby land to itself by law. What is this called? | Referendum | Occupation | Insignia | *Annexation
test: In «troops without insignia (badges)», what are insignia? | Boats | *Badges that show whose soldiers they are | Weapons | Maps of the country
exam: Which sentence best describes annexation? | A friendly team game played on a field | *One country taking land from another and making it part of itself | Mixing paints to make a new colour | A list of birthdays in a class
